    "notes": {
      "swiss": "Section 4 of the paper takes this annual private liability triangle from Gisler (2015), which states that the figures were adjusted for privacy reasons. The paper had no premium for it, so the authors simulated one from the observed ultimate claims under a 60 percent target loss ratio, reporting a resulting weighted-average ultimate loss ratio of 59.4 percent. The premium is therefore derived from the claims and is not an independent observation.",
      "liability": "Section 5 of the paper describes two quarterly incurred triangles that an insurer supplied to the authors. The claim figures were divided by a random number to preserve confidentiality, and the earned premium was normalised to a 50 percent average loss ratio across all development quarters. This is the long-tail liability class.",
      "property": "The second of the two quarterly incurred triangles described in Section 5 of the paper, on the same confidentiality and premium normalisation basis as the liability triangle. This is the short-tail property class, and its published cells include decreases, so some development increments are negative."
    },
